INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Zero-Day Vulnerability in MOVEit Transfer Exploited for Data Theft; NVD CVE-2023-34362", "source_url": "https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/cybersecurity-advisories/aa23-158a; https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/cve-2023-34362", "text": "Mandiant reported wide exploitation of a MOVEit Transfer zero-day announced by Progress on May 31, 2023 and assigned CVE-2023-34362. Earliest evidence of exploitation was May 27, 2023, involving web-shell deployment and data theft; some theft occurred within minutes. The vulnerability is an unauthenticated SQL injection in MOVEit Transfer, exploitable over HTTP/HTTPS, allowing database access and potentially SQL statements that alter/delete data; NVD lists CVSS 3.1 9.8 Critical and says it was exploited in the wild in May and June 2023. Attackers deployed a newly discovered C# LEMURLOOT web shell, commonly masquerading as human.aspx, with observed names human2.aspx and _human2.aspx. LEMURLOOT could enumerate files/folders, retrieve configuration and Azure Blob settings/credentials, download files, and create/delete a Health Check Service user. Scanning/exploitation often came from 5.252.188.0/22; second-stage hosts often exposed RDP and certificates generated May 19-22, suggesting staging, though this is an assessment. Mandiant initially attributed the activity to UNC4857, then on June 6 merged UNC4857 into FIN11 based on targeting, infrastructure, certificate, and data-leak-site overlaps. Mandiant noted overlaps with historical FIN11 infrastructure, including an IP used by FIN11 from mid-January 2023 and the same RDP x509 certificate. On June 5, Bleeping Computer reported a claimed CLOP affiliation; on June 6, the CL0P^_-LEAKS data leak site claimed responsibility and threatened publication unless an extortion fee was paid. Mandiant described the activity as reminiscent of prior FIN11-attributed data-theft extortion via the CL0P^_-LEAKS DLS, but said it had not directly observed extortion emails to confirmed victims. Mandiant assessed it plausible FIN11 delayed ransom emails; this is explicitly uncertain."}
